          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Measurable locales are certain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            much as general locales serve as a basis for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            (especially in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measurable+space"} localizable measurable space
            (or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+equivalence"} dual
            to a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebra"} commutative von Neumann algebra
          %p
            Ironically, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %em not
            locales (except for the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %p
            The concept is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"} Dmitri Pavlov
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            At present, there is no purely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+theory"} order-theoretic
            definition of measurable locales. However, there are a few other ways of defining them.
          %h3#in_classical_mathematics In classical mathematics
          %p
            First we give definitions appropriate for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
          %p
            As a preliminary step, consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Comp
                  %mi Bool
                  %msub
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi sup
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp Bool Alg_{sup}
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+boolean+algebras"} complete boolean algebras
            and
            = succeed "-preserving" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            of boolean algebras. This is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Frm
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Frm
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
            and so its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Comp
                  %mi Bool
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi sup
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp Bool Alg_{sup}^{op}
            is a full subcategory of the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loc"} Loc
            of locales. The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Meas
                  %mi Loc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
            of measurable locales is yet further a full subcategory of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                    %mi Bool
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi sup
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp Bool Alg_{sup}^{op}
            %em which
            complete boolean algebras are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of this category. There are at least three equivalent ways of doing so.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                By one definition, a complete boolean algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is a
                %strong measurable locale
                if there is a (complete) compact strictly
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measure+space"} localizable measure space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
                (as a boolean algebra) to the boolean algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi 𝒩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}/\mathcal{N}
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+subsets"} measurable subsets
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                modulo
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+subsets"} null subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is irrelevant except to specify the null subsets. In this way,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Meas
                      %mi Loc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
                becomes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
                to the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CSLEMS
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CSLEMS
                of (complete) compact strictly localizable
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enhanced+measurable+spaces"} enhanced measurable spaces
                %em complete
                boolean algebra as
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℳ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi 𝒩
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}/\mathcal{N}
                = succeed "," do
                  %em localizable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N}
                specified.)
            %li
              %p
                By another definition, a complete boolean algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is a
                %strong measurable locale
                if for every element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \neq 0
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                there is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+valuation"} continuous valuation
                (see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#normalmeasures"} below
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                valued in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(x) = 1
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi μ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt \mu(x) \lt \infty
            %li
              %p
                By yet another definition, a complete boolean algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is a
                %strong measurable locale
                if there is a commutative
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi W
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-star-algebra"} algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
                (as a boolean algebra) to the boolean algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Proj
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj(A)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection+operators"} projection operators
                in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+operator"} idempotent operator
                for a construction of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Proj
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj(A)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Meas
                      %mi Loc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
                becomes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+equivalence"} dual
                to the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Comm
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mo *
                      %mi Alg
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comm W^* Alg
                of commutative
                = succeed "-algebras." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi W
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi Proj
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \cong Proj(A)
                be complete (or even a boolean algebra); this can be proved.
          %p
            Whichever of these equivalent definitions is adopted, a
            %strong measurable function
            between measurable locales is simply a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            between them as locales; these are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Meas
                    %mi Loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            (thought of as measurable locales) is a function from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            (thought of as Boolean algebras) that preserves all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
            (and then it also preserves finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
            and so is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            that preserves all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
          %h3#in_constructive_mathematics In constructive mathematics
          %p (This section is not due to Pavlov.)
          %p
            For purposes of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Toby+Bartels"} Toby Bartels
            suspects that it is appropriate to use the definition from
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo ⋆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^\star
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+real+numbers"} lower real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/located+real+number"} located
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Proj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj(A)
            has
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+equality"} decidable equality
          %p
            For this definition (as was remarked above), we need not require that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Proj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \cong Proj(A)
            be complete. Constructively, we
            %em cannot
            require this;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Proj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj(A)
            need not be complete (although it is still a boolean algebra). Indeed, consider the point (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#examples"} the examples
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \coloneqq \mathbb{C}
            %em not
            the complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            of
            %em all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            but only the (possibly incomplete) boolean algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\bot, \top\}
            of
            %em classical
            truth values (corresponding to the self-adjoint idempotent complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %p
            It seems that there is some notion of completeness that applies here;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            should in some sense be ‘measurably complete’. In the point, for example, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{* \;|\; P\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a truth value, is measurable iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is true or false, so the supremum of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\top \;|\; P\}
            exists in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            under the same circumstances. Figuring this out could allow the definitions through measurable spaces or continuous valuations to work.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Meas
                    %mi Loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            boolean algebra with one element.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Meas
                    %mi Loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            boolean algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\bot, \top\}
            of (classical)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            is the boolean algebra of Lebesgue-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+sets"} measurable sets
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            modulo the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+sets"} null sets
          %p
            Applying the classification of
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
            (dually a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+product"} direct product
            of boolean algebras) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊤
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\bot, \top\}^I
            for a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            of arbitrary cardinality. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is finite, we get discrete spaces; if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is countable, we get the real line. Note that a single real line is already isomorphic to the union of countably infinitely many real lines. (Of course, we can't expect this
            %span.newWikiWord
              classification theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/classification+theorem"} ?
            to hold constructively.)
          %h3#borel_measurable_spaces Borel measurable spaces
          %p
            To do
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+sets"} Borel sets
            (rather than Lebesgue sets) as a measurable space. This is actually a little tricky, because
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            is not complete on the Borel sets, and passing to the Lebesgue sets gives a different measurable space. We might simply take the Borel sets as they are (so that only the empty set is null), but then this is not complete as a boolean algebra.
          %p
            One might suspect that there is no Borel real line in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Meas
                    %mi Loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+nonsense"} abstract nonsense
            that it must exist, and more generally that any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            (including the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale+of+opens"} locale of opens
            of any
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %strong Borel measurable locale
          %p
            …
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/31603/why-do-probabilists-take-random-variables-to-be-borel-and-not-lebesgue-measurab/31724#31724"} http://mathoverflow.net/questions/31603/why-do-probabilists-take-random-variables-to-be-borel-and-not-lebesgue-measurab/31724#31724
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor+theorem"} representable functor theorem
            …
          %p In the case of the real line, the resulting measurable locale is actually rather complicated: something like an uncountable disjoint union of points and lines.
          %p
            This process is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Loc
                    %mo →
                    %mi Meas
                    %mi Loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\colon Loc \to Meas Loc
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi Loc
                    %mo →
                    %mi Meas
                    %mi Loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top \to Loc \to Meas Loc
          %h3#lebesgue_measurable_spaces Lebesgue measurable spaces
          %p
            Similarly,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            give rise to (much simpler!) measurable locales by taking the Lebesgue sets (possible since we know which sets are null sets on a smooth manifold). This process is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi SmoothMan
                      %mi subm
                    %mo →
                    %mi MeasLoc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\colon SmoothMan_subm \to MeasLoc
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi SmoothMan
                    %mi subm
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SmoothMan_subm
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+map"} smooth
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submersions"} submersions
          %p
            In the case of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %em any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second-countable+space"} second-countable
            smooth manifold with positive dimension gives rise to this measurable locale; the really interesting thing about
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is what it does to the morphisms.
          %h2#normalmeasures Measures
          %p
            On
            %em any
            complete boolean algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+monoid"} abelian monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence+space"} convergence structure
            (such as
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+measure"} normal measure
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            valued in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu\colon L \to R
            such that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inclusion-exclusion"} additivity
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\bot) = 0
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∧
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo +
                        %mi μ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∨
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi μ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo +
                        %mi μ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(x \wedge y) + \mu(x \vee y) = \mu(x) + \mu(y)
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+continuity"} continuity
                \: if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is a downward-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codirected+set"} directed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infimum"} infimum
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+element"} bottom element
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/net"} net
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi μ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu(x))_{x\colon S}
                converges to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          %p
            One could, of course, define a garden-variety
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure"} measure
            by requiring continuity only when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the image of a decreasing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+valuations"} continuous valuations
          %p
            Absolutely continuous measures on, say, Lebesgue space correspond to normal measures on the real line as a measurable locale. (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            is not normal on the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            This should work in constructive mathematics, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
            (for a positive measure) being be the space of nonnegative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+reals"} lower reals
          %h2#references References
          %p The basic definition and some elementary properties are given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"}> Dmitri Pavlov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gelfand-type duality for commutative von Neumann algebras
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.05284"}> arXiv:2005.05284
                \.
          %p
            This paper also establishes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand-type+duality+for+commutative+von+Neumann+algebras"} Gelfand-type duality for commutative von Neumann algebras
          %p
            The theory of measurable locales originated on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MathOverflow"} MathOverflow
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"} Dmitri Pavlov
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Pavlov"}> Dmitri Pavlov
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/49426/is-there-a-category-structure-one-can-place-on-measure-spaces-so-that-category-th/49542#49542"} answer
              to
              %em Is there a category structure one can place on measure spaces so that category-theoretic products exist?
              (version: 2011-01-08)
          %p
            For a development of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            in this context, see
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Georg Lehner
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Georg+Lehner"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Measure theory via Locales
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08826"} arXiv
